hexagon: fuse the gate chains of the Qwen3.5 layers into two DSP ops
Fold-into: upstream/hexagon-htp

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 plus every patch of the series before this one.

The cost

Each layer of Qwen3.5 ends with an elementwise gate before its output projection:
  - A gated delta net layer: MUL(MUL(RMS_NORM(x), ssm_norm), SILU(z)), for each head of 128 values.
  - An attention layer: MUL(attn, SIGMOID(CONT(g))), where g is the gate half of the joint q projection.
HTP0 runs each chain as three ops that each read and write the full tensor in f32: RMS_NORM+MUL, SILU and
MUL, or CPY, SIGMOID and MUL. The per-op profile of the 4B (one prefill ubatch of 1024 tokens, the flags of
the app) gives 16.9 + 15.3 + 23.3 ms for the 24 gated delta net layers and 8.1 + 4.5 + 6.5 ms for the 8
attention layers. These ops run at the byte roofline (48 to 62 GB/s), thus only fewer bytes make them faster.
A decode token runs 96 such ops at about 4 us each.

The change

Two DSP ops (htp/gate-ops.c) do each chain in one pass over the rows:
  - HTP_OP_RMS_NORM_GATE (x, w, z): dst = (rms_norm(x) * w) * silu(z)
  - HTP_OP_SIGMOID_GATE (a, g): dst = a * sigmoid(g), where g can have any row strides
Each thread moves blocks of rows into the VTCM with a DMA double buffer. It computes a block with the row
routines of the unfused ops: hvx_fast_rms_norm_mul_f32 for each row, the SiLU and the sigmoid of
unary-ops.c, and hvx_mul_f32_aaa of the MUL. The last three give each value from its own inputs only, thus
they take all rows of a block in one call. The output has the bits of the unfused ops, for each row length
and each block size. The DDR traffic of a row falls from 7 row passes to 3 in each of the two chains (the
unfused chain reads or writes 2 + 2 + 3 rows).

The compute of one output vector on one thread (the lab, timing mode): 93.7 cycles for RMS_NORM_GATE (rows of
128 values: the norm 60.4, the SiLU 26.1, the MUL 7.5) and 31.3 for SIGMOID_GATE (rows of 256 values). The
DDR moves the 3 vectors of an output vector in about 14 cycles for all threads together, thus with 6
threads RMS_NORM_GATE is near the limit of its compute (about 16 cycles) and SIGMOID_GATE is at the limit of
the DDR. A copy of the norm routine with the same intrinsics in the same sequence for 4 rows together takes
48.6 cycles in place of 60.4, but with the flags of the DSP library (the release profile of the lab) the
copy did not give the bits of the routine: 1 or 2 ulp in 16 % of the values, and finite values in a row
where the routine gives NaN (a row with an Inf). The same source does not give the same bits in a
different code context, thus the op calls the routine itself.

The host (htp-gate-fusion.h) finds the two chains in graph_compute and puts the fused op at the index of
the last MUL. The op then reads its inputs later than the unfused ops did. In the graph of the model the z
projection of a gated delta net layer comes after the RMS_NORM, and without more work ggml-alloc gives the
bytes of the GATED_DELTA_NET output to that projection. Two parts keep the inputs intact:
  - graph_optimize asks the scheduler (add_alloc_dep) to keep each input of a chain allocated until the
    last MUL. The compute buffer of the 4B does not change (the hexhost graphs harness: 495.52 MiB with
    and without the fusion).
  - graph_compute checks the bytes of the allocated graph and rejects a chain when a node between the read
    of an input and the output writes a byte of the input, when the output shares bytes with an input and
    the rows are not the same, when an intermediate has a reader outside the chain or in a different split,
    or when no block of one row fits in the VTCM.
The environment switches GGML_HEXAGON_FUSE_NORM_GATE and GGML_HEXAGON_FUSE_ATTN_GATE are 1 when not set.
0 keeps the unfused ops, thus one library can do an A/B run. GGML_HEXAGON_OPFUSION=0 also keeps them.

test-backend-ops gets the cases NORM_GATE_FUSION and SIGMOID_GATE_FUSION: the 4B shapes at 1, 7, 64 and
1024 tokens, x as the head of a GATED_DELTA_NET output, rows with a gap, widths that are not a multiple of
32 values, the gate first in the MUL, a gate with no CONT, and a second reader of the activation (no
fusion).

The results on the host

The hexhost graphs harness (the host code with a fake DSP, the real graphs of the 4B Q8_0): the prefill,
decode, decode with 4 recurrent snapshots, MTP and image turn paths fuse each chain (24 RMS_NORM_GATE and
8 SIGMOID_GATE ops in each forward pass), and no candidate is rejected. Each forward pass sends 64 ops
less. With no allocation dependency the prefill rejects the 24 norm chains, because the z projection writes
the bytes of the GATED_DELTA_NET output. Thus the dependency is necessary, and the check finds the hazard.

The kernel lab (tools/htp-lab, target gate, functional mode, v79, release and debug flags): the fused op
against the unfused ops (RMS_NORM_MUL or RMS_NORM and MUL, SILU and MUL, or the CONT, SIGMOID and MUL), 26
cases with 1 to 6 threads, blocks of 1 row to the VTCM limit, rows of 1 to 256 values, strided x, the gate
first, and inputs with +-0, subnormals, 1e30, +-Inf, NaN and the band [14.49, 15.73] of the int16 SiLU:
each output value has the bits of the unfused ops.

The results on the phone

HTP0 of the OnePlus 13 (v79), one library set with the patch, the fusions on (no switch set) and off
(GGML_HEXAGON_FUSE_NORM_GATE=0 GGML_HEXAGON_FUSE_ATTN_GATE=0), the 4B Q8_0, 2026-09-24, thermal status 0 in
each run:
  - test-backend-ops -b HTP0 against the CPU: NORM_GATE_FUSION 9/9, SIGMOID_GATE_FUSION 9/9 and SIGMOID_MUL
    13/13, on and off. With the op profile, the runs with the fusions on show 8 RMS_NORM_GATE ops, 8
    SIGMOID_GATE ops and 9 SIGMOID_GATE ops (the SIGMOID_MUL layouts that the match takes).
  - The logits of the 4B (the FNV-1a hash of each llama_decode, hexhost_logits_hash): 32 decode steps, a
    prefill of 1024 tokens, and 16 steps of the MTP draft path give the same hash with the fusions on and
    off. Thus the logits are bit exact.
  - The HTP0 compute buffer does not change: 992.04 MiB (decode), 990.00 MiB (prefill), 992.04 and 130.03
    MiB (MTP), on and off.
  - llama-bench, the flags of the app (flash attention, Q8_0 K and V, n_batch = n_ubatch = 1024, 4 threads,
    GGML_HEXAGON_OPFUSION=1, GGML_HEXAGON_OPFUSION_STATE=1), 3 rounds with the two variants alternated. The
    change is the median of the ratio on/off of the rounds, and the ratios of the 3 rounds follow it:
      pp512  d0     931.3 against 901.5 t/s  +3.9 %  (+2.5, +4.2, +3.9 %)
      pp1024 d0     898.9 against 872.9 t/s  +3.5 %  (+5.0, +3.5, +1.4 %)
      tg32   d0     10.56 against 10.54 t/s  -0.1 %  (-0.2, -0.1, +0.4 %)
      tg32   d4096  10.22 against 10.16 t/s  +0.5 %  (+0.5, +0.6, +0.3 %)
    The samples of one tg32 run at d0 spread by 2 %, thus the decode change at d0 is in the noise.

